# One Root: The Session Identity Architecture

### Every key in Session derives, by public computation, from a single 128-bit seed — and the system's other secrets mostly aren't

**Anonymous security research — November 3, 2026**
**Status: vendor notified the same day. The vendor's response, if any, will be appended to this document unedited.**

- ---

## Abstract

Session is marketed as an anonymous, metadata-resistant messenger whose accounts are
ordinary Ed25519 identities. A complete read of the cryptographic code that ships in the
current releases shows a different structure. Every account's master key is produced from
a **16-byte seed padded with 16 zero bytes** — 128 bits of entropy — and **every other key
in the system is computed from that seed by public, labeled hash derivations**. There is
no ratchet, no session state, no negotiated secret anywhere in the design: nothing that
one party knows and can destroy, rotate, or revoke. A single recovery-phrase compromise
is therefore total and retroactive against every ciphertext of that account ever retained
by anyone.

Around that root, several subsystems give up more than the root alone would: synced
configuration messages are encrypted **deterministically** and carry a **hash of their own
plaintext appended in the clear**, turning every retained config ciphertext into an offline
oracle that verifies plaintext guesses with no key; the "blinded" identities used in
public communities are reversible to real Session IDs with **one multiplication using the
server's public key**; and the default push mode delivers **full message bodies to the
foundation's push server**, contradicting the project's published FAQ.

Each claim below is reproducible from the shipped code. Method and version pins follow;
nothing in this document depends on an old snapshot.

## Scope and method

- - Native core: **libsession-util**, as shipped in the Android application via the Maven
  artifact `org.sessionfoundation:libsession-util-android:1.1.0-58-gc5c6c89`
  (source pin `1d565e31`). Current Android HEAD still pins this exact artifact
  (`gradle/libs.versions.toml:33`).
- - Clients: Android **1.33.7** (2026-10-14) and iOS **2.15.5** (2026-10-11) — the latest
  releases as of November 3, 2026.
- - Source HEADs re-fetched November 3, 2026: libsession `dac149cd`, Android `a8011ac`,
  iOS `195c9fb`. **The eight crypto-relevant core files are byte-identical between the
  shipped pin and current HEAD** (config encryption, session encryption, config
  protobuf delivery, attachments, blinding, group keys, ed25519, multi-encryption).
- - The blinded-ID reversal (§5) was additionally demonstrated live against a running
  community server on November 2, 2026, recovering a real Session ID byte for byte.
- - No user's account was accessed. No tooling was built against any user. All analysis is
  static source review plus self-computed derivations.

## 1. The root is 128 bits, and it is the only secret

Account creation generates a 16-byte seed and pads it to the 32 bytes Ed25519 expects:

- - Android, `LoggedInState.kt:69`: the account key is built from `seed.data + ByteArray(16)`;
  `:99` sets `SEED_LENGTH = 16`; `:106–107` confirms the 16 bytes themselves come from
  `SecureRandom` — the randomness is fine; there is simply half as much of it as the
  key size advertises.
- - iOS, `Identity.swift:47,51`: the seed is extended with `Data(repeating: 0, count: 16)`
  before key construction. The project's own comment at `Identity.swift:162` states it
  plainly: *"Our account is generated with a 16-byte seed where the second 16-bytes are
  just padding…"*
- - The Pro master key is derived from the same padded seed (Android
  `LoggedInState.kt:85`), and the account's long-term identity on the payment backend is
  that same master public key.

128 bits is not brute-forceable, and this document does not claim otherwise (§7). The
problem is not the size alone. The problem is that **the seed is the only secret the
system has**. Signal-protocol systems survive key-compromise events because most of
their working keys are ephemeral, ratcheted, and destroyed. Session has no such layer:
every key below is a deterministic function of this one value, so the compromise of one
short string — a recovery phrase is exactly that string, written in words — compromises
all of them, past and future, with no mechanism to rotate, revoke, or even detect it.

## 2. The derivation map: everything is a public function of the root

- From the single seed, by labeled BLAKE2b derivations whose labels and inputs are printed
in the source:

- - **Account identity** — the Ed25519 key pair itself (§1).
- - **Configuration sync keys** — derived per config type from the seed (`key_base`), then
  per-message by message size and domain (§3).
- - **Deterministic message ephemerals** for shared-config delivery (§4).
- - **Attachment keys and nonces** — from the seed and the file's content (§5).
- - **Community blinded identities** — from the seed and the server's public key (§6).
- - **Group keys, all generations** — from the group seed and the member list; every past
  and future generation's encryption key and nonce are recomputable by anyone who has
  ever held the group seed.
- - **File-server "version" key pair** — a second, permanent Ed25519 key pair derived from
  the seed, presented to the file server on every version check and upload: a stable
  per-account pseudonym at that server, linkable across all of a user's activity there.
- - **Pro payment identity** — provider-side account identifiers are deterministic
  functions of the master public key, structurally joining Session identity to
  payment-provider records held by the operator.

There is no item on this list with independent entropy. (One honorable exception found
in the audit: the push `notificationKey` is independently random — generated with
`SecureRandom` — and is therefore also the only key in the system that never rotates,
including across the legacy-to-current account migration.)

A useful way to state the consequence: **you do not decrypt Session; you re-derive it
and compare.** Every ciphertext in the system doubles as an offline verifier for
whoever obtains the corresponding seed, and retained copies — on service nodes, in
push infrastructure, in backups, in legal process returns — never expire, because the
keys that open them cannot change.

## 3. Configuration encryption is an offline guessing oracle

Device-sync configuration messages (profile, contacts, conversation settings — the
account's own description of itself) are encrypted as follows
(`src/config/encrypt.cpp`, pin and HEAD identical):

- - Key: `blake2b(key_base ‖ message_size ‖ domain)`, where `key_base` derives from the
  account seed.
- - Nonce: a keyed hash **of the plaintext itself**, under a public domain string
  (`"libsessionutil-config-encrypted-" + domain`). The format's own header
  (`include/session/config/encrypt.hpp:66`) documents that this exists so that
  *"different clients will encrypt the same data to the same result"* — determinism is
  the design goal, for cross-device deduplication.
- - The 24-byte nonce is **appended to the ciphertext in the clear** (total overhead 40
  bytes: 16-byte tag + 24-byte nonce).

Consequences, no key required:

1. Anyone holding a retained config ciphertext can test any guessed plaintext offline:
   recompute the hash, compare with the appended nonce. A match confirms the guess.
   Low-entropy fields — a display name, a profile picture hash, a contact set — are
   dictionary-attackable against ciphertexts the attacker never had the key for.
2. Encryption is fully deterministic: identical configs produce identical ciphertexts,
   linkable across time and across every server that stores them.

## 4. Shared-config delivery uses a recomputable "ephemeral"

Message encryption for shared configuration delivery uses
`encrypt_for_recipient_deterministic` (`src/session_encrypt.cpp:134`). The ephemeral
key pair — the component whose secrecy is the entire point of an ephemeral — is seeded
by a keyed hash (`"SessionBoxEphemeralHashKey"`, `:111`) over:

> sender's seed ‖ recipient's **public** key ‖ plaintext

(`:145`, in the code's own comment.) The in-tree caller is shared-config delivery
(`src/config/protos.cpp:93`); the surrounding code carries the annotation
*"NOTE: This is dumb"* twice.

Anyone who obtains a sender's seed recomputes the ephemeral secret for every such
message that sender ever sent, from public information plus the plaintext — which §3
already makes guessable. Ordinary one-to-one direct messages use the random-ephemeral
path and are **not** affected by this specific construction (§7); the deterministic
construction covers configs, group-config delivery, and attachments (§5) — the
metadata-richest traffic in the system.

## 5. Attachments: keys from seed and content, by design

Attachment encryption (`include/session/attachments.hpp:89–109`,
`src/attachments.cpp:218–242`) derives the key and nonce by keyed BLAKE2b over the
uploader's seed and the file content. The header documents the purpose: server-side
deduplication. The consequences follow directly: the same user uploading the same file
produces the same ciphertext forever; there is one permanent server-side copy; and
possession of the seed at any later date opens every attachment the account ever
uploaded, with the content hash structure providing its own confirmation.

## 6. Community "blinded" IDs reverse with the server's public key

Public communities do not show real Session IDs; they show blinded IDs, presented to
users as an anonymity layer. Two generations exist. The one communities actually run
is **blind15**: the blinding factor is `scalar_reduce(blake2b(server_pk))`
(`src/blinding.cpp:26`) — computed **from the server's public key alone**, a value the
server publishes and every client fetches.

Reversal is one scalar multiplication by the modular inverse of that factor. It was
demonstrated live on November 2, 2026: a blinded ID taken from a running community was
unblinded and recovered the underlying real Session ID byte for byte. (The exact
primitive matters: recovery succeeds with `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_noclamp` and
fails with the clamped variant — an implementation detail any replicator will hit
within the hour.)

The operational meaning: the operator of any community server — and anyone who obtains
its stored message table, in which posts sit readable at rest, encryption having never
been applied to room content — holds a complete, identified roll of everyone who has
posted, under their real, permanent Session IDs. A blinded ID is not a pseudonym. It
is the real ID, multiplied by a public number.

The stock application no longer *displays* blinded IDs in its interface. They remain
present in every community protocol exchange. Hiding a value from the user interface
is not a cryptographic property.

## 7. Push: full message bodies flow to the operator, by default, hard-coded

Session's FAQ states, verbatim:

> "When using fast mode neither Apple nor the STF can see the contents of your
> messages, who you're talking to, or exactly when messages are sent or received"

Fast mode is the default. In fast mode, the service-node swarm streams message bodies
to the Session Push Notification Server, which wraps them under a per-user encryption
key — a key that lives in the push operator's own subscription database, alongside the
subscriber's Session ID, push token, namespaces, and rolling monitoring signatures.
The clients do not offer the alternative the protocol itself specifies (blank wake-up
pushes): Android hard-codes the data subscription on
(`PushRegisterApi.kt:50`: `data = true, // only permit data subscription for now (?)`),
and iOS signs the same choice (`SubscribeRequest.swift:55`, production caller passes
`true`).

To be precise about what is and is not shown: the push database schema contains no
message-body table, so **permanent retention of bodies is not claimed**. Transit
custody is total: content, identity, token, and timing meet in one operator's hands,
on the default path, for effectively every user — the exact conjunction the FAQ
denies. The App Store listing meanwhile carries the labels "No metadata logging" and
"Data Not Collected."

## 8. What this document does not claim

Honesty about bounds is part of the evidence, and the bounds are these:

- - **2^128 is not brute-forceable** with any known or plausibly foreseeable classical
  capability. No account is being opened by guessing. The failure is that one 128-bit
  string is the *only* secret, in a system architecturally incapable of recovering
  from its compromise — not that the string is guessable.
- - **Ordinary one-to-one message content** uses pairwise constructions with random
  ephemerals (the multi-recipient encryption path was read in full and is clean). The
  deterministic failures are in configs, group-config delivery, and attachments.
- - The **push notificationKey is independently random** and not seed-derived.
- - The **ONS name system** resolves names to Session IDs through a publicly computable
  decryption by design; it exposes nothing that is not its stated purpose.

The claims in §§1–7 require none of the exceptions to bend. They are complete at the
level stated.

## 9. Impact

Compose the findings and the system's posture is this: a Session ID — a public value,
shared by design with every correspondent and every community — is a complete map of
where an account's data lives and how it is keyed; the account's own configuration
ciphertexts verify guesses about the account without any key; the anonymity layer of
its public communities is a public multiplication away from a permanent identity; its
default notification path hands message content to the operator the FAQ says cannot
see it; and the single secret underneath all of it is half the advertised size and
can never be changed.

This is not a hypothetical population. The project's co-founder's Session ID is
public — posted by the project itself, for years, as a contact point. Every derivation
described here applies to that account, today, by anyone, exactly as it applies to
every community user who believes a blinded ID hides them. There is no exemption at
the top of the org chart, because the mathematics does not contain one.

## 10. What a fix requires

This architecture cannot be patched incrementally; it has to be re-founded. In outline:

1. Full-entropy (32-byte) account seeds, with a migration that treats every existing
   recovery phrase as permanently sensitive.
2. Independent, secret per-purpose key material — real session state, ratcheting, and
   forward secrecy for message content, in place of the current static derivations.
3. Randomized configuration encryption; plaintext-derived nonces removed; stored
   ciphertexts re-encrypted under the new scheme and the old copies' sensitivity
   disclosed to users.
4. Community identity that is not a public function of the account identity and a
   public server key — blind25-class constructions at minimum, with room content
   protected at rest.
5. The blank-push mode the protocol already specifies, shipped and defaulted, or the
   FAQ corrected to describe what fast mode actually does.

Each is a known construction. None is a patch release.

## Disclosure

The vendor was notified directly on November 3, 2026, before publication, and offered
the full analysis and a place for its response in this document. That offer stands:
any response from the Session Technology Foundation will be appended, unedited, with
its receipt date, to every canonical copy of this paper.

**Correspondence log.**

- - *2026-11-03* — Kee Jeffreys, co-founder (his Session ID is the contact ID the
  project itself publishes), notified directly and replied the same day. In substance:
  he no longer works on Session and directed us to the current team for an official
  response; he conceded that blind15 blinded IDs can be resolved to Session public
  keys, stated that the issue is addressed in the blind25 protocol — while noting he
  was unsure of client support status (at current HEAD, community authentication
  remains blind15; §6) — and characterized the remaining findings as incorrect or
  misleading without identifying a specific error. Any specific error identified by
  him or the team will be corrected in this document, publicly, with the correction
  credited.
- - Notification to the current Session Technology Foundation team follows, per his
  redirect.

